f","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":6,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"I've read the packet, the full diff and the executor's log, and I'm approving PKT-0094.\n\n**Checks against the packet**\n- **Contract:** nothing in core changed. `git -C external/core diff refs/theseus/base..HEAD` is empty, and all 7 changed files are under `.theseus/oracle/differential` or in packet bookkeeping.\n- **Budget:** 447 added lines across 7 files, under the limits of 1500 lines and 8 files.\n- **Goal:**\n  - `x2t.sh` builds x2t from `external/core.base` and from `external/core`, cached by git tree, and checks that each build actually runs.\n  - **Direct comparison:** both builds convert the same inputs the ways the server does (open, save, own format, PDF, ODF), interleaved and repeated. Outputs are compared by content: normalized XML inside each package, and PDF page count plus extracted text.\n  - **Browser comparison:** the script swaps each build into the running Document Server in turn, under a lock, and puts the installed x2t back when it exits. Chromium then drives user A's local edits and user B's co-editing edits, then the save that combines both, the conversions and the reopen.\n  - **What gets compared:** the document model, what each user sent the other, what is shown, the saved and converted files, and the reopened model.\n  - **Timing:** both sides get timing lines, and differences listed in `x2t.intended` are allowed.\n- **Last review's points:** all five are now addressed (browser-driven edits with the combined save, a check that each build runs, the original side built from core.base, content comparison, repeated timing).\n- **Rejection rules (TEP-0004):** the Executor log is filled in. No follow-up packets were requested, and none are listed. The one surprise, a random spell-check underline, is handled in the runner's `stableShown()` (spell check off, then wait for 3 identical captures), and the reason is recorded in the log and the Lessons.\n- **Performance rule:** this packet builds an oracle and doesn't aim to make anything faster, so the rule about rejecting when there's no measured gain doesn't apply. The timing figures are noise between identical builds, as expected.\n\n**Weak spots that don't block approval**\n- **Untested failure path:** core and core.base are at the same commit, so the oracle has never been run against a real change to core. The executor says so openly.\n- **Coarse PDF check:** only page count and text are compared, so a layout-only change in a PDF wouldn't be caught.\n- **What is shown:** only user A's screen is compared, with spell check off. That hides spell-check rendering, which is acceptable since that rendering isn't produced by x2t.\n- **Co-editing messages:** they are compared as object id, change type and byte length, not by their full bytes. A change that keeps a record the same length would slip through, though the model and saved-file checks would likely still catch it.\n- **Stale capture:** if what is shown never settles within 30 seconds, `stableShown()` returns its last capture instead of failing. That could cause a flaky difference rather than a clear error.\n\nVERDICT: APPROVE oracle meets the goal (content-based direct + browser co-editing differential, cached builds, timing) with no change to core; the earlier review's points are addressed","ttft_ms":8008,"type":"result","duration_ms":35625,"uuid":"6f6c64ee-6b5e-4d6d-ad9b-33f97a3ad295","ttft_stream_ms":6475,"time_to_request_ms":339,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":7,"system_prompt":232,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":19,"input_hooks